WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:02.802 align:start size:97% position:3%
- We had to turn to the
federal government for help,

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:05.472
to state government for help.

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:09.109 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've stood in line
for government cheese.

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:10.410
I've stood in line for bread.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.012
And I knew at 11 years old,

00:00:12.012 --> 00:00:13.980
that that was not
America's best,

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.782
that there was more for us.

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:18.418 align:start size:84% position:16%
We didn't live that
way in Florida.

00:00:18.418 --> 00:00:22.455
We didn't have to pay with
monopoly money for groceries

00:00:22.455 --> 00:00:24.557
in the grocery store.

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:27.494 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's what we
were doing in Colorado.

00:00:27.494 --> 00:00:31.131
My stepdad, he ended up
getting work in Aspen

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:32.565
when I was about 12,

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:35.201 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we moved to the
Western Slope of Colorado.

00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:36.503 align:start size:81% position:19%
And that really
was the best thing

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:38.338 align:start size:81% position:19%
that could've ever
happened to us.

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:42.208
We moved into a community
that wasn't just telling us

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:45.478
where to get a handout, but
they were offering a hand up.

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.847
(soft music)

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:56.589 line:0%
- All right, I'm Dave Rubin,
and we are live in studio today

00:00:56.589 --> 00:01:00.460 line:0%
with the Congresswoman from
Colorado's third district,

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.295
Lauren Boebert, welcome
to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:03.763 line:0%
- Thanks so much for
having me on Dave.

00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:07.000 line:0%
This is fantastic to actually
be in person with someone,

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.701 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
talking face-to-face,
imagine that.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.170
- Face-to-face, we've got what?

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:11.404 align:start size:97% position:3%
About four feet between us?
- Yes. (laughs)

00:01:11.404 --> 00:01:12.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Are we in any sort
of legal trouble?

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:14.541 align:start size:88% position:13%
Could this get you
kicked out of Congress?

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:15.742
What are we doing here?

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:16.976 align:start size:81% position:19%
- According to
political science,

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.978 align:start size:84% position:16%
we are in deep trouble
right now. (laughs)

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:20.013
- What about regular science?

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:21.181
I know political science,

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:22.682 align:start size:97% position:3%
we are in a lot of trouble.
- Yes.

00:01:22.682 --> 00:01:24.317
I think we're okay with
regular science. (laughs)

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:25.251
- All right.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:26.920
We got you all the way out

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.421 align:start size:84% position:16%
to crazy California.
- Yes.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:29.789
- You're here for
a couple of days,

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.324
how do you feel in California?

00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:34.594
You come from the, I would
say relatively free state

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.529 align:start size:88% position:13%
of Colorado, or the
ones really free state,

00:01:36.529 --> 00:01:38.198
sort of hanging on.

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:39.933
- Yes, we are hanging on.

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:43.570 align:start size:97% position:3%
I do believe that there are
more conservatives in Colorado

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.072
than the average
person would know.

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:49.375 align:start size:84% position:16%
We are slowly turning
into California.

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:51.778
And really, it's been
quicker than any of us

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.480
would have liked to have seen.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.748 align:start size:84% position:16%
But we have a governor
from California

00:01:55.748 --> 00:01:58.952 align:start size:97% position:3%
who wants to mimic everything
that's happened here.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's been documentaries
that have gone out,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:02.088
the "Rocky Mountain Heist."

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:04.624
There's a book, "The
Colorado Blueprint."

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.660
And they were kind of
sounding the alarms

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.462 align:start size:84% position:16%
of what was happening,
and that Colorado

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:13.833
was really a petri dish for
all of these liberal policies

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:18.838
that Democrats want
to take nationwide.

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:20.673 align:start size:81% position:19%
And if they can
do it in Colorado,

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.709 align:start size:84% position:16%
they can do it
anywhere successfully.

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:23.943
And so that's what we're seeing.

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:26.212 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we didn't have any
voices in Colorado.

00:02:26.212 --> 00:02:29.949
So I'm really happy to be
that loud voice in Colorado

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:31.217
right now, to let everyone know

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:32.719
what's happening to us there.

00:02:32.719 --> 00:02:34.554
- Yeah, and I've done a
couple events in Colorado

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.456 align:start size:97% position:3%
over the last couple of
years, although unfortunately,

00:02:36.456 --> 00:02:38.258
none in the last
year and a half,

00:02:38.258 --> 00:02:39.726
but when I was on tour
with Jordan Peterson,

00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:41.261
I went and a few other things.

00:02:41.261 --> 00:02:43.429
And what I noticed when I
talked to a lot of people

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:46.266
was "Dave, Dave,
could you make sure

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:48.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
"all the California
people stop coming here?"

00:02:48.268 --> 00:02:49.469
- Yes, yes.

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:50.770 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Are you still hearing
a lot about that?

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:52.238
- Yes, we still have
Californians coming,

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:54.707
and these political
refugees are bringing

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:56.509
their bad policies with them.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:59.612 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so, that's really
devastating to our communities

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:01.948
because we like our
rural way of life,

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:05.652 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we don't want
that urban lifestyle.

00:03:05.652 --> 00:03:06.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
Hey buddy!
- My dog has decided

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:07.754
to say hi. (laughs)

00:03:07.754 --> 00:03:09.989
- Hi! (laughs)

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.591
I have dogs, I've goats

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:13.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
that bring so much joy.
- We are not editing this out,

00:03:13.159 --> 00:03:15.628
but Clyde does have
to leave. (laughs)

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:16.930
- So sweet.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:17.864
- Clyde!

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:18.798
Clyde just opened the door.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:20.300
- Oh, hello.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.467
- He's distracting

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:22.402 align:start size:88% position:13%
the congresswoman.
- You love me. (laughs)

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:23.603
- We could be in trouble here.

00:03:23.603 --> 00:03:25.505 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, we have the
outtake for the show.

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:27.507
Clyde, you gotta go now.

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:29.909
Guys, we have a tricked or a...

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:30.944
(Lauren laughs)

00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:32.679
- [Man] Sorry!

00:03:32.679 --> 00:03:33.880
- That brings me a lot of joy.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:35.582
I love animals.

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:37.016
(crosstalk drowns the speakers)

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:38.484
Yes, wild. (laughs)

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:39.619
- Yeah.

00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:40.853 align:start size:88% position:13%
So people are constantly
opening the door

00:03:40.853 --> 00:03:41.788
and breaking into
your interviews.

00:03:41.788 --> 00:03:42.755
I think, sorry, you were saying.

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:44.390
- That's fine. (laughs)

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:48.027
I dunno where we were, but
with everything that's...

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:49.295
Where were we?

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:50.797
- We were talking about
the Californians coming.

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:53.433
- Yes, and they're bringing
their bad policies with them.

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:56.936
And so there really is an
urban and rural divide,

00:03:56.936 --> 00:04:00.640
and we want to keep
rural Colorado rural.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
But unfortunately, in these
high mountain ski resort towns

00:04:03.643 --> 00:04:05.111
that I represent,

00:04:05.111 --> 00:04:07.580 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of Californians
can afford to move there,

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they're bringing
the same policies

00:04:09.282 --> 00:04:10.917
that they're fleeing from.

00:04:10.917 --> 00:04:14.120
- So I ask this to
every conservative or
Republican official

00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.723
that I talk to, like
when the people come,

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:19.025
do you think they have
no idea that they voted

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:21.127
for those policies
that are now fleeing,

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:23.796
and realize that maybe they
should think about things

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:25.098
a little bit differently now?

00:04:25.098 --> 00:04:25.932
- No.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.935 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because their actions
would prove that,

00:04:28.935 --> 00:04:31.237 align:start size:88% position:13%
they think they can flee
from their problems.

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:36.242
And unfortunately, there
is a lower class of people

00:04:37.377 --> 00:04:38.878
who cannot afford to
flee those bad policies

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:41.381
that they have voted
in and implemented.

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:44.751
And so they get to leave,
pack up and go to somewhere

00:04:44.751 --> 00:04:47.353
with lower taxes and
better opportunities,

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:50.056 align:start size:84% position:16%
and still have a
beautiful scenic view.

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:52.892 align:start size:88% position:13%
But there is another
class that's left behind

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:55.128
that can not afford to
make that same move,

00:04:55.128 --> 00:05:00.133
and they're the ones who are
stuck in that terrible economy,

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:03.636
and a horrible state
where they are left now

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:08.308
to depend on the government
to help in their situations,

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:12.412 align:start size:88% position:13%
or even in these failing
economies where taxes

00:05:12.412 --> 00:05:15.114
are just too high
to afford the homes

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:16.416
that they wanna live in.

00:05:16.416 --> 00:05:20.320
So, it's really impacts
them more than anything.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.422
And then they come
to these other states

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:23.790
and bring those same policies.

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:27.126 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I wouldn't think that
they knew what impact

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.861
they're having on
these communities.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, the idea that
they're leaving the people

00:05:30.863 --> 00:05:33.733
who can't afford to move
is sort of interesting too.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:34.967 align:start size:88% position:13%
I realized this morning
when I was thinking

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.337 align:start size:88% position:13%
about how I wanted to
do this interview, that,

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:40.440
if you take sort of the new
crop of Congress people,

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you think about
mostly the Democrats side,

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:44.410 align:start size:84% position:16%
we all know
everything about AOC.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:46.112
We know everything
about Ilhan Omar,

00:05:46.112 --> 00:05:47.780 align:start size:84% position:16%
probably too much more
than we wanna know.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:49.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
We know everything
about Ayanna Pressley,

00:05:49.515 --> 00:05:50.783
and the rest of the crew,

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:52.452 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we don't actually
know that much

00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:54.354 align:start size:84% position:16%
from a national
perspective about you.

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:55.621
And it's like, it
seems like people

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:58.424 align:start size:88% position:13%
should be talking
about you just as much,

00:05:58.424 --> 00:06:00.026
but that's not really
how the media operates

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:02.428 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause you're a
scary conservative.

00:06:02.428 --> 00:06:03.596
- Scary conservative.

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:06.733 align:start size:84% position:16%
I should be the
Democrats dream story.

00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:11.337
I was raised a Democrat, and
I was dependent on government.

00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:13.272
And I thought I always would be,

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:15.141
because when you're stuck
in that cycle of poverty,

00:06:15.141 --> 00:06:17.076
it's really hard to
break free of it.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:18.344 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, can you tell everyone
your story a little bit?

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:19.946
- Yeah. Absolutely.

00:06:19.946 --> 00:06:22.515
So I was born in
Orlando, Florida.

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:25.151
My mom was single, we
lived with her parents.

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:29.622
My grandparents, I attribute
a solid foundation to them,

00:06:29.622 --> 00:06:33.893 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the few years that
they've had me in their home.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:36.362
But my mom just up and
moved us to Colorado

00:06:36.362 --> 00:06:37.997
when I was four years old.

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:42.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
She met a guy and moved
to an apartment in Aurora.

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:45.471
And I think her
pride was too big

00:06:45.471 --> 00:06:47.740
to go back home to her parents.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.776
Things kinda got a little rough.

00:06:49.776 --> 00:06:51.677
He wasn't the prince charming

00:06:51.677 --> 00:06:53.746
that she thought
he was going to be.

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:56.416 align:start size:97% position:3%
But then eventually, she
had my first younger brother

00:06:56.416 --> 00:07:00.186
with him, and she didn't
want to have another child

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:01.554
not have a father.

00:07:01.554 --> 00:07:05.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, we were
stuck, we were here,

00:07:05.858 --> 00:07:08.428
through the good, the
bad and the very ugly.

00:07:08.428 --> 00:07:12.932
And that led us to times
where he wasn't working.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:16.269 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we had to turn to the
federal government for help,

00:07:16.269 --> 00:07:18.938
to state government for help.

00:07:20.406 --> 00:07:22.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
I've stood in line
for government cheese.

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:23.943
I've stood in line for bread.

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:25.478
And I knew at 11 years old,

00:07:25.478 --> 00:07:27.447
that that was not
America's best,

00:07:27.447 --> 00:07:29.282
that there was more for us.

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:31.851 align:start size:84% position:16%
We didn't live that
way in Florida.

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:34.987
We didn't have to pay
with monopoly money

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:38.057
for groceries in
the grocery store.

00:07:38.057 --> 00:07:40.960 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that's what we
were doing in Colorado.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.597 line:0%
My stepdad, he ended up
getting work in Aspen,

00:07:44.597 --> 00:07:46.032 line:0%
when I was about 12,

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:48.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we moved to the
Western Slope of Colorado.

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:49.936 align:start size:81% position:19%
And that really
was the best thing

00:07:49.936 --> 00:07:51.771 align:start size:81% position:19%
that could've ever
happened to us.

00:07:51.771 --> 00:07:55.641
We moved into a community
that wasn't just telling us

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:59.145
where to get a handout, but
they were offering a hand up.

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:02.415
And they started sharing with
my mom conservative values.

00:08:02.415 --> 00:08:05.685 align:start size:97% position:3%
So she listened and even
changed her voter registration

00:08:05.685 --> 00:08:09.555
to Republican, but she
didn't vote Republican.

00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:11.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, that's a surprise.
- Yes, yes.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:14.827
She got to the ballot box
and said, "No, I'm scared.

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:19.165
"I'll lose everything if
Republicans are in charge."

00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:20.933 align:start size:84% position:16%
We didn't have enough
stability for her

00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:23.102
to let go of that mindset.

00:08:23.102 --> 00:08:27.006 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, I started
working at 15 years old

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:29.475
at the Rifle McDonald's,
and I can still remember

00:08:29.475 --> 00:08:31.711 align:start size:84% position:16%
bringing mom home
that first paycheck.

00:08:31.711 --> 00:08:33.813 align:start size:84% position:16%
I remember the
dignity that had come

00:08:33.813 --> 00:08:36.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
with earning a paycheck,
putting my hand to something

00:08:36.716 --> 00:08:37.984
and creating wealth.

00:08:37.984 --> 00:08:39.952
And that's something
that never left me.

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:42.154
I learned at a very young age
that I could do a better job

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:44.790
taking care of myself than
the government ever had.

00:08:44.790 --> 00:08:48.728
So I began to develop these
conservative principles,

00:08:48.728 --> 00:08:50.796
not even knowing
what was happening.

00:08:50.796 --> 00:08:55.668 align:start size:97% position:3%
And my husband and I, we met
when he moved to Colorado

00:08:55.668 --> 00:08:57.837
to work in the oil
and gas industry.

00:08:57.837 --> 00:09:02.775
And, we knew that we could
build a great life together,

00:09:03.643 --> 00:09:05.144
a successful life together.

00:09:05.144 --> 00:09:07.280 align:start size:84% position:16%
I had only ever
wanted to be a mom.

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:10.816
I didn't have dreams
to be a business owner,

00:09:10.816 --> 00:09:15.421
to be a doctor or a lawyer,
certainly not a politician.

00:09:15.421 --> 00:09:16.856
I just wanted to be a mom.

00:09:16.856 --> 00:09:19.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
My mom was my hero and
someone that I looked up to.

00:09:19.559 --> 00:09:23.229
And so whenever we, when we
started our life together,

00:09:23.229 --> 00:09:25.765 align:start size:84% position:16%
we wanted to start
our family right away.

00:09:25.765 --> 00:09:29.502
And that left me with a very
difficult decision to make,

00:09:29.502 --> 00:09:30.803
my senior year of high school,

00:09:30.803 --> 00:09:33.539
'cause now I have a
child and a family,

00:09:33.539 --> 00:09:35.775 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I didn't wanna go
back to the bread lines.

00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:39.078 align:start size:88% position:13%
I didn't want government
cheese for my boy.

00:09:39.078 --> 00:09:41.180
And so I quit high school,

00:09:41.180 --> 00:09:43.983
and I took a management
position with McDonald's.

00:09:43.983 --> 00:09:47.420
That was very good for my age,
and that was more valuable...

00:09:47.420 --> 00:09:49.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Wait, they took you
into a management position

00:09:49.655 --> 00:09:50.957 align:start size:88% position:13%
after quitting high
school, just like that?

00:09:50.957 --> 00:09:51.791
- Yes.

00:09:51.791 --> 00:09:52.992
- Wow.

00:09:52.992 --> 00:09:53.693
- Yes, no degree
necessary. (laughs)

00:09:53.693 --> 00:09:54.627
- Wow.

00:09:54.627 --> 00:09:55.461
Wow. (laughs)

00:09:55.461 --> 00:09:56.495
- Yes.

00:09:56.495 --> 00:09:57.663
Well, it was really...

00:09:57.663 --> 00:09:59.198 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was the hours
that I needed to work

00:09:59.198 --> 00:10:00.900
for that shift manager position

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:02.602
of why I needed to
quit high school.

00:10:02.602 --> 00:10:05.037 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was working nights
with them, and they said,

00:10:05.037 --> 00:10:07.506
"To be a shift manager, we
need you in this day shift."

00:10:07.506 --> 00:10:11.644
And so I had to leave high
school to take on that role.

00:10:11.644 --> 00:10:16.649 align:start size:97% position:3%
And for me, there was more
value in earning that paycheck

00:10:17.984 --> 00:10:19.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
for my family than
sitting in geography class

00:10:19.452 --> 00:10:21.520 align:start size:97% position:3%
for another two months until
I graduate high school.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.389
- Do you remember what
you were making per hour,

00:10:23.389 --> 00:10:24.223
at that point?

00:10:24.223 --> 00:10:25.291
- 45 a year.

00:10:25.291 --> 00:10:26.125
- 45 A year?

00:10:26.125 --> 00:10:26.993
- 45,000 a year.

00:10:26.993 --> 00:10:28.260
- Yeah.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:29.528
Oh, that's once you were
in the manager position.

00:10:29.528 --> 00:10:30.963
But do you remember what
you were making per hour

00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:31.797
at McDonald's before that?

00:10:31.797 --> 00:10:33.566
- About 7.50.

00:10:33.566 --> 00:10:36.335 align:start size:84% position:16%
There was several
different pay periods

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:37.536
that I had with them.

00:10:37.536 --> 00:10:39.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
I started out, I
think at 6.15 an hour,

00:10:39.872 --> 00:10:41.941 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then I was
about 7.50 an hour.

00:10:41.941 --> 00:10:44.243
And then I went to
a salaried position.

00:10:44.243 --> 00:10:46.579 align:start size:84% position:16%
- How much of that
shapes your economics,

00:10:46.579 --> 00:10:47.546
the way you think about it now?

00:10:47.546 --> 00:10:49.081
Because to be a good person,

00:10:49.081 --> 00:10:50.750
I thought we have to
have everyone making,

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:53.119 align:start size:84% position:16%
or the lowest people
have to make $15.

00:10:53.119 --> 00:10:54.754
That means you're a
good moral person.

00:10:54.754 --> 00:10:55.988
If you scream that,

00:10:55.988 --> 00:10:57.990 align:start size:88% position:13%
should you have been
making $15 an hour then?

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:01.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
- No, I worked for every
extra dollar that I did earn.

00:11:01.694 --> 00:11:04.697
And it really gave
me an opportunity

00:11:04.697 --> 00:11:07.366
that I wouldn't have had
if minimum wage was $15.

00:11:07.366 --> 00:11:09.902 align:start size:88% position:13%
Look at how many things
are automated now?

00:11:09.902 --> 00:11:12.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now that we're
reaching $15 per hour,

00:11:12.405 --> 00:11:16.709
McDonald's has kiosks instead
of the 15, 16, 17 year old

00:11:16.709 --> 00:11:19.311
high school student
that was coming in

00:11:19.311 --> 00:11:20.546
and earning a paycheck.

00:11:20.546 --> 00:11:22.848
And so I would not have
had the opportunities

00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:26.052 align:start size:97% position:3%
that have shaped my
worldview, my economic view

00:11:26.052 --> 00:11:27.586
had minimum wage been so high,

00:11:27.586 --> 00:11:29.789
because they would not
have been able to afford

00:11:29.789 --> 00:11:33.459 align:start size:97% position:3%
to invest in me and provide
that opportunity for me

00:11:33.459 --> 00:11:38.164
to have a work ethic and
develop my life skills.

00:11:38.164 --> 00:11:39.932
- So I actually didn't know
that part of your story,

00:11:39.932 --> 00:11:42.168 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's a pretty
perfect through line

00:11:42.168 --> 00:11:43.969
to you own a restaurant now.

00:11:43.969 --> 00:11:45.471
- Yes, yes I do.

00:11:45.471 --> 00:11:47.373 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So, you were doing something
right back in high school

00:11:47.373 --> 00:11:48.741
at McDonald's?

00:11:48.741 --> 00:11:51.010 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yes, I'm a very
spontaneous person by nature.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:53.446
Sometimes I see it as
my greatest downfall,

00:11:53.446 --> 00:11:56.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
but sometimes it
works out pretty well.

00:11:56.382 --> 00:11:58.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I ended up
leaving McDonald's,

00:11:58.784 --> 00:12:01.287
and I went into the oil and
gas industry for awhile.

00:12:01.287 --> 00:12:04.190 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was filing some papers
at a local company,

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:06.425
and then decided to go out
in the field and do work,

00:12:06.425 --> 00:12:08.627
and I was a pipeline technician.

00:12:08.627 --> 00:12:12.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
So locating pipeline,
doing cathodic protection

00:12:12.031 --> 00:12:14.100
without an engineering degree.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:17.837 align:start position:0%
It's possible to do work without
a four-year college degree.

00:12:17.837 --> 00:12:21.073
And so I was locating pipelines.

00:12:21.073 --> 00:12:23.476 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was GPSing the pipelines,
making maps to them,

00:12:23.476 --> 00:12:24.744
doing cathodic protection,

00:12:24.744 --> 00:12:29.515
which is we install
anodes to protect

00:12:29.515 --> 00:12:31.517
the pipe from corroding.

00:12:31.517 --> 00:12:34.754 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so it was just this
fascinating part of my job

00:12:34.754 --> 00:12:37.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I loved and got to
drive all around the valley,

00:12:37.189 --> 00:12:39.058
and hike up and down mountains,

00:12:39.058 --> 00:12:41.727 align:start position:0%
locating these pipes and making
sure they were protected.

00:12:41.727 --> 00:12:44.296
And then the oil
and gas industry

00:12:44.296 --> 00:12:47.433
started to be heavily regulated.

00:12:47.433 --> 00:12:50.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
Colorado saw the
industry pulling out.

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:52.772
So a lot of us, our
days were numbered.

00:12:52.772 --> 00:12:55.941 align:start size:97% position:3%
I ended up leaving and decided
to be a stay-at-home mom.

00:12:55.941 --> 00:12:59.211
I had three boys then and said,

00:12:59.211 --> 00:13:01.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm gonna just be with
my boys for awhile.

00:13:01.413 --> 00:13:05.651
And unfortunately, a little
bit later, it got worse.

00:13:05.651 --> 00:13:08.521
And so my husband ended up
running out of contracts,

00:13:08.521 --> 00:13:10.756 align:start size:88% position:13%
and he wasn't working
in the industry anymore.

00:13:10.756 --> 00:13:13.025
And we said we needed a plan B.

00:13:13.025 --> 00:13:16.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was a local
restaurant that shut down,

00:13:16.362 --> 00:13:18.364
and it was one of our favorites.

00:13:18.364 --> 00:13:22.535
It was called The Cowboy
Calf-A, C-A-L-F dash A.

00:13:22.535 --> 00:13:24.170
- Ah, got it, got it.

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:26.605
- Yes, and they closed down

00:13:26.605 --> 00:13:29.008 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they had a for rent
sign in the window.

00:13:29.008 --> 00:13:32.344
And we drove by one
day and said, maybe,

00:13:32.344 --> 00:13:33.846
maybe we could do this.

00:13:33.846 --> 00:13:36.916 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so we went in, it was a
great deal to lease the place,

00:13:36.916 --> 00:13:38.450
the equipment was there.

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.319
And we decided whenever
he went back to work

00:13:40.319 --> 00:13:43.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
to start investing his
paycheck into the restaurant

00:13:43.389 --> 00:13:44.890
and opening it up.

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:47.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
Very spontaneous, never
owned a restaurant before,

00:13:47.193 --> 00:13:50.129 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I wanted a way to
give back to my community.

00:13:50.129 --> 00:13:52.298 align:start size:84% position:16%
A lot of our friends
were unemployed,

00:13:52.298 --> 00:13:54.533 align:start size:81% position:19%
and they needed
opportunity again.

00:13:54.533 --> 00:13:56.869 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that opportunity
wasn't there

00:13:56.869 --> 00:13:59.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the oil and gas
industry like it had been.

00:13:59.405 --> 00:14:02.708
And so we opened up, we
hired our waitresses,

00:14:02.708 --> 00:14:04.443
our kitchen staff,

00:14:04.443 --> 00:14:06.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we were just a
Western-themed restaurant

00:14:06.979 --> 00:14:10.049
wanting to give excellent
service to our community.

00:14:10.049 --> 00:14:12.718
And it was a pretty great place.

00:14:14.119 --> 00:14:15.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
When we first opened, I
didn't have a lot of money.

00:14:15.621 --> 00:14:17.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
I didn't have any
money for advertising.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:21.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
So we went door to door on
the first day to each business

00:14:21.026 --> 00:14:23.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
and said, hey, come in and
have a free meal on us.

00:14:23.596 --> 00:14:26.866
And that was cheaper than any
advertising I could have done.

00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:29.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it worked, people liked
it, and they were coming back.

00:14:29.969 --> 00:14:32.304 align:start size:94% position:6%
So what you may have heard
about the restaurant,

00:14:32.304 --> 00:14:35.708 align:start size:97% position:3%
a few weeks after we opened
our Western-themed restaurant,

00:14:35.708 --> 00:14:39.345 align:start size:97% position:3%
there was an altercation where
a man was brutally beaten

00:14:39.345 --> 00:14:40.813
outside of the restaurant.

00:14:40.813 --> 00:14:45.017
Show up in the morning, there's
crime scene tape everywhere,

00:14:45.017 --> 00:14:46.518
there's police officers.

00:14:46.518 --> 00:14:48.654
I was talking with some of
the officers that I knew

00:14:48.654 --> 00:14:50.556
asking what had happened.

00:14:50.556 --> 00:14:53.592
And there was a man who
was beaten pretty badly,

00:14:53.592 --> 00:14:55.327
and he ended up losing his life.

00:14:55.327 --> 00:14:58.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so that immediately
prompted the question to me,

00:14:58.264 --> 00:14:59.932
how am I gonna take
care of everyone?

00:14:59.932 --> 00:15:03.168
How am I going to protect the
people that are around me?

00:15:03.168 --> 00:15:04.837
So, I went across the street,

00:15:04.837 --> 00:15:09.275
and talked to a local gun
dealer, a gun salesman,

00:15:09.275 --> 00:15:12.678
and I asked him about
Colorado's carry laws.

00:15:12.678 --> 00:15:15.881 align:start position:0%
And he said, "Well, you can
carry openly without a permit."

00:15:15.881 --> 00:15:16.949
So that's what I did.

00:15:16.949 --> 00:15:18.317
I began to open carry.

00:15:18.317 --> 00:15:20.552 align:start size:97% position:3%
Soon after that, my waitresses
began to open carry.

00:15:20.552 --> 00:15:22.988
And the next thing you know,
Shooters Grill was being called

00:15:22.988 --> 00:15:24.657 align:start size:84% position:16%
the safest restaurant
in America.

00:15:24.657 --> 00:15:27.192
- Shooters Grill, so
there is the name.

00:15:27.192 --> 00:15:28.494
And there have been no shootings

00:15:28.494 --> 00:15:29.695 align:start size:81% position:19%
at Shooters Grill?
- No.

00:15:29.695 --> 00:15:31.130 align:start size:84% position:16%
- People are not
murdering each other

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:32.631 align:start size:84% position:16%
or anything like that?
- No.

00:15:32.631 --> 00:15:34.300
- It's interesting because
you mentioned at the top

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:36.936 align:start size:97% position:3%
that your story, the
Democrats should sort of love

00:15:36.936 --> 00:15:40.205
your story, but in a way, I
get why they hate your story.

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:42.708 align:start size:84% position:16%
You came from nothing,
you worked hard,

00:15:42.708 --> 00:15:43.909
you did a bunch of stuff.

00:15:43.909 --> 00:15:45.945
You kept growing, you
care about family.

00:15:45.945 --> 00:15:47.112
- Right.

00:15:47.112 --> 00:15:48.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That's a problem for them.
- Yes.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:49.415
- They can't have
the success story.

00:15:49.415 --> 00:15:50.649
- Right.

00:15:50.649 --> 00:15:53.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
They are okay with you
turning a blind eye

00:15:53.352 --> 00:15:54.553
to their failures.

00:15:54.553 --> 00:15:58.190 align:start size:84% position:16%
If the man in The
White House has a son

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:00.492
who's lost three laptops,

00:16:00.492 --> 00:16:04.163 align:start size:88% position:13%
and is addicted to
smoking Parmesan cheese,

00:16:04.163 --> 00:16:06.365
we can turn a blind eye to that.

00:16:06.365 --> 00:16:11.236
If a CNN analysis gets caught

00:16:11.236 --> 00:16:13.706 align:start position:0%
doing some very, very
horrendous, inappropriate things

00:16:13.706 --> 00:16:16.709 align:start size:97% position:3%
on a Zoom meeting, then that's
okay as long as he comes on

00:16:16.709 --> 00:16:18.610
and says, "I'm sorry."

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:22.614
But no, that real life,
when things get tough,

00:16:22.614 --> 00:16:27.219 align:start size:97% position:3%
you get tougher, and you
make success out of failures.

00:16:27.219 --> 00:16:29.621 align:start size:94% position:6%
You learn from your
mistakes and you continue.

00:16:29.621 --> 00:16:33.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
You don't let that hold
you back or keep you down,

00:16:33.692 --> 00:16:37.262 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you offer to other
people a way to have success

00:16:37.262 --> 00:16:38.130
in their lives.

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:39.832
That is what they hate.

00:16:39.832 --> 00:16:42.267 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So when did you decide,
"Okay, I've got these ideas,

00:16:42.267 --> 00:16:44.937 align:start size:88% position:13%
"my life's in order now,
restaurant's working

00:16:44.937 --> 00:16:47.373
"and got the family, let
me get into politics,"

00:16:47.373 --> 00:16:48.574
of all things?

00:16:48.574 --> 00:16:50.809
(Dave and Lauren laugh)

00:16:50.809 --> 00:16:52.044
"Let me throw that all away."

00:16:52.044 --> 00:16:52.978
- Right.

00:16:52.978 --> 00:16:54.913
Well, being in the restaurant,

00:16:54.913 --> 00:16:56.382
a lot of people come in,

00:16:56.382 --> 00:16:58.217
they're complaining about
the issues of the day.

00:16:59.585 --> 00:17:03.789 align:start position:0%
I think you and I could probably
relate with being involved

00:17:03.789 --> 00:17:06.258
in Republican politics,

00:17:06.258 --> 00:17:08.193
because we're both
kind of new to that.

00:17:09.628 --> 00:17:12.264
I voted Republican, but
I wasn't paying attention

00:17:12.264 --> 00:17:16.402
to who I was voting for,
why I was voting for them,

00:17:16.402 --> 00:17:18.904 align:start size:88% position:13%
and what happened in
between those elections.

00:17:18.904 --> 00:17:22.274 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so I'm actually fairly
new to that process.

00:17:22.274 --> 00:17:24.977
I just know, as
a business owner,

00:17:24.977 --> 00:17:26.478
they were affecting my business.

00:17:26.478 --> 00:17:28.380
They were affecting my family.

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:29.648 align:start size:84% position:16%
They were affecting
the paychecks

00:17:29.648 --> 00:17:31.150
that we were able to bring home.

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:35.621
I was electing people who I
thought had my same views,

00:17:35.621 --> 00:17:37.456
but then they would
get to Washington DC

00:17:37.456 --> 00:17:40.659 align:start size:84% position:16%
or to The State
Capitol, overregulate,
over tax, overspend

00:17:40.659 --> 00:17:43.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
and destroy everything that
we're trying to build at home.

00:17:43.395 --> 00:17:46.265 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so, we talk about that
kinda stuff in the restaurant.

00:17:46.265 --> 00:17:50.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I really believe that a
lot of our problems in America

00:17:50.869 --> 00:17:54.706
is because we had an entire
generation at least say,

00:17:54.706 --> 00:17:57.342
you're not allowed to talk
about religion or politics.

00:17:57.342 --> 00:18:00.345 line:0%
And now we have an entire
generation that is ignorant

00:18:00.345 --> 00:18:03.982 line:0%
about the things of
God and government.

00:18:03.982 --> 00:18:05.184 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you're not allowed
to have that...

00:18:05.184 --> 00:18:06.185
- They've made politics
religion, basically.

00:18:06.185 --> 00:18:08.287
- Yes, yes, exactly.

00:18:08.287 --> 00:18:10.522 align:start size:88% position:13%
That is their God,
government is their God.

00:18:10.522 --> 00:18:13.225
And that's why they believe
that government can come in

00:18:13.225 --> 00:18:15.394
and rule you and
you need to submit,

00:18:15.394 --> 00:18:17.096 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they can take
rights away from you

00:18:17.096 --> 00:18:20.432 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they are the
granter of those rights.

00:18:20.432 --> 00:18:21.266
- [Dave] Speaking my language.

00:18:21.266 --> 00:18:22.301
- Yes. (laughs)

00:18:22.301 --> 00:18:23.235
Yes.

00:18:23.235 --> 00:18:24.536
And so, in my restaurant,

00:18:24.536 --> 00:18:26.171
you're free to talk
about those things,

00:18:26.171 --> 00:18:27.673
even if you disagree with me.

00:18:28.874 --> 00:18:31.243
Freedom of speech is so
under attack right now,

00:18:31.243 --> 00:18:35.114
and if we lose that,
we lose everything.

00:18:35.114 --> 00:18:36.882
Hate speech is free speech.

00:18:38.016 --> 00:18:40.486 align:start size:88% position:13%
Speech that you disagree
with is free speech.

00:18:40.486 --> 00:18:43.422 align:start size:88% position:13%
And this is something
that we have to protect.

00:18:43.422 --> 00:18:45.557
So this isn't just a
Second Amendment issue

00:18:45.557 --> 00:18:48.760 align:start size:94% position:6%
or a 10th Amendment
issue, this is everything,

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:51.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
our livelihoods and
everything moving forward.

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:56.001
We know what Big Tech is doing
to sensor freedom of speech.

00:18:56.001 --> 00:19:00.439 align:start size:97% position:3%
And really, it's the first
time in history that Big Tech,

00:19:00.439 --> 00:19:03.942
somewhat an entity this
big is more of a threat

00:19:03.942 --> 00:19:05.444
than big government,

00:19:05.444 --> 00:19:08.814 align:start size:97% position:3%
because this is someone
playing the role of government

