"And it was really hard work.

00:57:35.785 --> 00:57:40.457
"One week, in seven days, I
worked a record 95 hours."

00:57:40.457 --> 00:57:42.926
I was there 95 hours, what?

00:57:42.926 --> 00:57:44.394
Like that's nothing.

00:57:44.394 --> 00:57:46.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I get on there and
talk to him and said, sir,

00:57:46.596 --> 00:57:48.264 align:start size:84% position:16%
I too remember my
first part-time job,

00:57:48.264 --> 00:57:49.699
(Dave laughs)

00:57:49.699 --> 00:57:51.601 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I'm not in this committee
room bragging about it.

00:57:51.601 --> 00:57:53.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
But he went on to
say that he has seen

00:57:53.837 --> 00:57:57.140 align:start size:94% position:6%
artificial
hyperinflation take place

00:57:57.140 --> 00:57:59.175
when these companies
come into communities,

00:57:59.175 --> 00:58:02.145
I live in a community
where we had a boom,

00:58:02.145 --> 00:58:03.880 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that boom didn't
have to go away.

00:58:03.880 --> 00:58:05.715 align:start size:88% position:13%
I've seen these schools
built, the roads,

00:58:05.715 --> 00:58:10.086
the fire departments, the
hospitals, the colleges,

00:58:10.086 --> 00:58:12.856 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the small
businesses that opened,

00:58:12.856 --> 00:58:14.557
my small business that opened.

00:58:14.557 --> 00:58:18.094
And we were able to flourish,
and having mud on your floor

00:58:18.094 --> 00:58:20.630 align:start size:84% position:16%
meant that things were
really going good.

00:58:20.630 --> 00:58:23.366
And people were coming in and
patronizing your business.

00:58:23.366 --> 00:58:25.802
I saw moms not have to work,

00:58:25.802 --> 00:58:27.270
because dad had a
good paying job.

00:58:27.270 --> 00:58:28.805
They could raise their babies.

00:58:28.805 --> 00:58:30.573
I was that mom for awhile.

00:58:30.573 --> 00:58:35.445
And I've also seen
politicians step in

00:58:35.445 --> 00:58:38.248 align:start size:94% position:6%
and regulate our
communities into poverty.

00:58:38.248 --> 00:58:40.316
That is artificial.

00:58:40.316 --> 00:58:42.919 align:start size:88% position:13%
Nothing about the
success was artificial,

00:58:42.919 --> 00:58:45.922
but that regulation is,
it didn't have to happen.

00:58:45.922 --> 00:58:50.627
And they want to outsource all
of this to China and Africa.

00:58:50.627 --> 00:58:53.663 align:start size:84% position:16%
In Africa, we have
40,000 children mining

00:58:53.663 --> 00:58:56.733 align:start size:88% position:13%
with their bare hands
for cobalt in the Congo,

00:58:57.700 --> 00:58:59.102
selling it to China,

00:58:59.102 --> 00:59:02.438 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we are buying
solar panels from them.

00:59:02.438 --> 00:59:04.774 align:start size:84% position:16%
These not in my
backyard extremists

00:59:04.774 --> 00:59:08.311 align:start size:97% position:3%
don't understand what's
happening with these policies,

00:59:08.311 --> 00:59:10.747
and what condition
they're creating

00:59:10.747 --> 00:59:12.181
for people in other countries.

00:59:12.181 --> 00:59:14.317 align:start size:97% position:3%
And they think that they're
doing something great,

00:59:14.317 --> 00:59:15.151
but they're not.

00:59:15.151 --> 00:59:16.352
- Right.

00:59:16.352 --> 00:59:17.854 align:start size:81% position:19%
Or they really
literally mean it,

00:59:17.854 --> 00:59:19.088
"As long as it's not in my
backyard, I don't care."

00:59:19.088 --> 00:59:19.923
- [Lauren] Yes.

00:59:19.923 --> 00:59:20.857
- At some level.

00:59:20.857 --> 00:59:21.791
That's the intentions part.

00:59:21.791 --> 00:59:23.493
- Yes, no, you're exactly right.

00:59:23.493 --> 00:59:27.463
I actually met a very, very
wealthy woman in New York.

00:59:27.463 --> 00:59:32.468
And there was a, she
wasn't my biggest fan,

00:59:33.937 --> 00:59:36.239
but there was a wind farm that
was going to be put literally

00:59:36.239 --> 00:59:38.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
in her backyard,
right across the road.

00:59:38.675 --> 00:59:41.177 line:0%
Furious, "Move it down the road.

00:59:41.177 --> 00:59:42.211 line:0%
"Why don't you put it down here?

00:59:42.211 --> 00:59:43.713 line:0%
"There's nothing over there.

00:59:43.713 --> 00:59:45.582 line:0%
"Why don't you put it in this
neighborhood, not in mine."

00:59:45.582 --> 00:59:47.750
I said, well, ma'am
you voted for this.

00:59:47.750 --> 00:59:48.985
This is what you wanted,

00:59:48.985 --> 00:59:50.653
but you don't want
it in your backyard.

00:59:50.653 --> 00:59:51.487
- It's for other people.

00:59:51.487 --> 00:59:52.555
- Yes!

00:59:52.555 --> 00:59:53.823
- All right.

00:59:53.823 --> 00:59:55.024
I think I only got
one more for you,

00:59:55.024 --> 00:59:56.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is so with
all of this in mind,

00:59:56.492 --> 00:59:58.261 align:start position:0%
I think a lot of people are
suddenly talking about this idea

00:59:58.261 --> 01:00:01.564
that the future of America is
just going to be the states

01:00:01.564 --> 01:00:03.433
kinda going their separate way.

01:00:03.433 --> 01:00:06.302
That just if you live
in Florida or Texas,

01:00:06.302 --> 01:00:09.238
it's gonna be very different
than if you live in California.

01:00:09.238 --> 01:00:12.241
Maybe that's already the
way it is, you in Colorado,

01:00:12.241 --> 01:00:14.911
or somewhere kinda
in between on that.

01:00:14.911 --> 01:00:17.313
But do you see that sort
of as just an inevitability

01:00:17.313 --> 01:00:18.548
and maybe that's okay?

01:00:18.548 --> 01:00:20.650 align:start size:88% position:13%
Maybe that's sort of how
the federalist system

01:00:20.650 --> 01:00:22.919
was supposed to work
in the first place?

01:00:22.919 --> 01:00:24.387
- I do think that it's okay.

01:00:25.655 --> 01:00:28.257 align:start position:0%
I don't want this authoritarian
federal government.

01:00:28.257 --> 01:00:30.960 align:start position:0%
I wanna shrink the federal
government as much as possible.

01:00:30.960 --> 01:00:32.128 align:start size:81% position:19%
I don't think that
there should be

01:00:32.128 --> 01:00:34.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
a federal department
of education.

01:00:34.330 --> 01:00:36.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
I want the federal
government out of our lives.

01:00:36.733 --> 01:00:41.671
And we do need to get back to
states having ultimate rule.

01:00:41.671 --> 01:00:45.008
Unfortunately, you do get
people in these positions

01:00:45.008 --> 01:00:47.644 align:start size:84% position:16%
where they seem to
have unchecked powers.

01:00:47.644 --> 01:00:49.078
They have emergency orders,

01:00:49.078 --> 01:00:51.681
and they're able to shut
down small businesses.

01:00:51.681 --> 01:00:56.653 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're able to
stifle innovation and
tax their citizens

01:00:59.756 --> 01:01:01.090
to a point of poverty.

01:01:01.090 --> 01:01:03.026
And so you've seen
it in California.

01:01:03.026 --> 01:01:05.028
We're starting to
see it in Colorado.

01:01:05.028 --> 01:01:07.930 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, that's obviously
the negative aspect

01:01:07.930 --> 01:01:11.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
of having states have
their own rights,

01:01:11.300 --> 01:01:13.469
is because it can be
a really bad place

01:01:13.469 --> 01:01:15.838
if you have bad people
making these decisions.

01:01:15.838 --> 01:01:18.207
But then you do have the
right to move somewhere

01:01:18.207 --> 01:01:19.475
that is more free.

01:01:19.475 --> 01:01:21.978
Look, I don't wanna have
to move to South Dakota,

01:01:21.978 --> 01:01:24.614
but I will move to
America if I have to.

01:01:24.614 --> 01:01:25.548
And so...(laughs)

01:01:25.548 --> 01:01:27.050
- I can read the subtext here.

01:01:27.050 --> 01:01:28.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're looking at me like,
yeah the weather's nice

01:01:28.251 --> 01:01:29.619 align:start size:88% position:13%
in Cali today, but what
are you doing here?

01:01:29.619 --> 01:01:31.087
I can see it.

01:01:31.087 --> 01:01:33.923 align:start position:0%
But you see that not necessarily
as a totally negative thing

01:01:33.923 --> 01:01:35.558
though, because then it
does give the opportunity

01:01:35.558 --> 01:01:36.426
for the other states?

01:01:36.426 --> 01:01:37.627
- Absolutely.

01:01:37.627 --> 01:01:39.829 align:start size:84% position:16%
There definitely needs
to be competition

01:01:39.829 --> 01:01:41.064
between these states.

01:01:41.064 --> 01:01:44.767
Let states compete
for their citizens,

01:01:44.767 --> 01:01:47.537
to have the best people
move to their state.

01:01:47.537 --> 01:01:49.605
There's a lot of bright
minds moving to Texas

01:01:49.605 --> 01:01:51.374
and to Florida right now.

01:01:51.374 --> 01:01:53.876
Companies moving
to these states,

01:01:53.876 --> 01:01:58.014
and they're going to make
these states better places.

01:01:58.014 --> 01:01:59.449
We have Magpul,

01:01:59.449 --> 01:02:01.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
they used to solely
be in Colorado,

01:02:01.784 --> 01:02:05.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they moved to
Wyoming because their
gun laws are better.

01:02:05.388 --> 01:02:08.825
And they are the number
two employer in Wyoming.

01:02:08.825 --> 01:02:11.360 align:start size:97% position:3%
They wanna come back to
Colorado, he said, "We can't,

01:02:11.360 --> 01:02:12.995
"your gun laws are outrageous.

01:02:12.995 --> 01:02:16.699
"It's near impossible to
have a business there.

01:02:16.699 --> 01:02:20.169
"And the way were starting
to be taxed and regulated,

01:02:20.169 --> 01:02:21.671
"so we're in Wyoming.

01:02:21.671 --> 01:02:23.806
"And when things change, let
us know, we'll come back."

01:02:23.806 --> 01:02:26.109 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Tesla just left
Cali to go to Texas.

01:02:26.109 --> 01:02:27.110
- Yes!

01:02:27.110 --> 01:02:28.311
- So there might
be a theme here.

01:02:28.311 --> 01:02:29.746
- Exactly.

01:02:29.746 --> 01:02:33.616 align:start position:0%
And so I would love for Colorado
to be an example one day,

01:02:33.616 --> 01:02:36.285
of how free we can be.

01:02:36.285 --> 01:02:41.290
Bringing energy development
back, and doing it cleanly.

01:02:43.292 --> 01:02:44.927
We have that ability there,

01:02:44.927 --> 01:02:47.196 align:start size:84% position:16%
nobody produces energy
better than we do.

01:02:47.196 --> 01:02:52.201 align:start size:97% position:3%
And to have everything that
our founders envisioned

01:02:53.402 --> 01:02:55.772
for our country right
there in one state,

01:02:55.772 --> 01:02:57.406
I would love to see
that for Colorado,

01:02:57.406 --> 01:02:59.742
and many more states all
throughout our nation.

01:02:59.742 --> 01:03:02.879 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Congresswoman, your
work is cut out for you.

01:03:02.879 --> 01:03:04.113
But I wish you the best of luck.

01:03:04.113 --> 01:03:05.047
And I'm glad we
finally did this.

01:03:05.047 --> 01:03:06.682
- Yes!

01:03:06.682 --> 01:03:09.285
- We met once in 2019
at a Turning Point
thing for a second,

01:03:09.285 --> 01:03:10.486
and we agreed to do a show,

01:03:10.486 --> 01:03:12.054 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then we basically
waited two years

01:03:12.054 --> 01:03:13.322
because we wanted
to do it in person.

01:03:13.322 --> 01:03:14.490
So I thank you for coming in.

01:03:14.490 --> 01:03:15.491
- Thank you so much, Dave.

01:03:15.491 --> 01:03:16.959 align:start size:72% position:28%
- Yeah.
- Thank you.

